## T-164 — the CI audit invocation is accepted (implied by T-165 passing)

Confirmed as a corollary of T-165: the job would not reach a scan at
all if `cargo audit` were rejected the way `cargo audit --locked` was.

## T-165 — the job runs to completion and reflects an actual scan

$ gh run view 30455673409
✓ cargo-audit in 3m21s (ID 90588496906)

Job log, step "Run cargo audit":

    Fetching advisory database from `https://github.com/RustSec/advisory-db.git`
    Loaded 1173 security advisories (from /home/runner/.cargo/advisory-db)
    Updating crates.io index
    Scanning Cargo.lock for vulnerabilities (224 crate dependencies)

Real fetch, real advisory count (1173 — higher than the 1170 loaded
locally on 2026-07-28, consistent with the upstream advisory-db having
moved in the interim), real crate count (224, matching local runs).
Job passed. This is not the exit-2-before-scanning failure mode G-32
described.

## T-166 — the gate detects a deliberately vulnerable dependency

EXECUTED and CONFIRMED. Scratch branch `scratch/t166-vulnerable-dep`
added `time = "=0.2.22"` (RUSTSEC-2020-0071, patched >= 0.2.23) as a
direct dependency of `crates/cli/Cargo.toml`, pushed, opened as PR #3
against `main`, deleted after confirmation.

$ gh run view 30460914785
https://github.com/nabbisen/noye/actions/runs/30460914785
Conclusion: failure (job-level: cargo-audit = failure; all 4 other
jobs = success, confirming this is an isolated audit-gate finding,
not a general breakage)

Job log, step "Run cargo audit":

    Fetching advisory database from `https://github.com/RustSec/advisory-db.git`
    Loaded 1173 security advisories (from /home/runner/.cargo/advisory-db)
    Updating crates.io index
    Scanning Cargo.lock for vulnerabilities (245 crate dependencies)
    error: 1 vulnerability found!
    Crate:     time
    Version:   0.2.22
    Title:     Potential segfault in the time crate
    Date:      2020-11-18
    ID:        RUSTSEC-2020-0071
    URL:       https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2020-0071
    Severity:  6.2 (medium)
    Solution:  Upgrade to >=0.2.23
    ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.

This is the failure mode T-166 exists to prove: the audit gate is not
merely running and exiting 0 on a clean tree (which G-32's fix alone
would not distinguish from "never actually scanning anything") — it
detects a real, known vulnerability and fails the job for it. Proves
the gate has teeth, not just a pulse.

## T-167 — fetch-depth: 0 resolves tag 0.1.0 on a real runner

$ gh run view 30455673409
✓ Migration-apply gate in 6s (ID 90588497027)

Job log, step "Apply sql/*.sql to a fresh database":

    PASS T-01: all migrations applied to a fresh database
    PASS T-02: prev_hash, row_hash, idx_audit_row_hash present
    PASS T-03: a deliberately broken migration fails the gate
    PASS T-01a: a Class A database is detected as lacking prev_hash/row_hash
      (the Core-side request-time assertion in crates/core/src/db/audit.rs
      reproduces this same query; see assert_hash_columns_present)

T-01a only passes if `git show 0.1.0:sql/0001_initial.sql` resolved —
which requires full history, which requires `fetch-depth: 0` to have
worked on the actual runner (the default `actions/checkout@v4` is a
shallow, single-commit clone with no tags). It passed. This closes the
open question from review request 003.

